{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["(vv. 9–11) <b>YHWH will establish you,</b> etc. From here until ״יִפְתַּח ה׳ לְךָ אֶת אוֹצָרוֹ הַטּוֹב״ is the promise of good that corresponds to the observance of negative commandments, whose root is in the word ״לִשְׁמוֹר״, and that is what the verse concludes: <b>for you shall observe the commandments of YHWH.</b> And its saying <b>and you shall walk in His ways</b> is to say that YHWH commanded only concerning things from which He distances Himself. And this promise is directed against the observance of negative commandments, for by virtue of a person guarding himself from transgressions he is called holy, as it is written ״קְדוֹשִׁים תִּהְיוּ״, and the meaning of the verse is the things through which they will be holy: ״אִישׁ אִמּוֹ״ etc., and ״אֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתַי״ etc., ״אַל תִּפְנוּ אֶל״ etc., and the rest of the admonitions stated there. And also according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Leviticus Rabbah 24:6) that concerning the admonition against forbidden relations it is said ״קְדוֹשִׁים״ — behold, this is included in לִשְׁמוֹר. Therefore it says, corresponding to the observance of negative commandments: ״יְקִימְךָ ה׳ לוֹ לְעַם קָדוֹשׁ״. And it was precise in saying the word <b>lo</b> to hint that Israel will be in the place of the angels whom HaShem selected for Himself to stand before Him, who are called holy, as it is written (Daniel 8:13) ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֶחָד קָדוֹשׁ״. HaShem will establish us in their place as a holy people, and the angels will be secondary to them, and so it says (Numbers 23:23) ״כָּעֵת יֵאָמֵר לְיַעֲקֹב״ etc. (Numbers Rabbah 20:20)." "And its saying <b>״וְרָאוּ כָּל עַמֵּי הָאָרֶץ״</b> etc. — this too is directed against the observance of negative commandments, for as long as a person guards himself from transgressions, the image of Elohim does not depart from his face, and all created beings, man and beast, tremble before him; unlike one whose deeds are loathsome, for they cause the image to depart from him, as Cain said (Genesis 4:14) ״וְהָיָה כָל מוֹצְאִי״ etc." "<b>And YHWH will make you superabundant for good in the fruit of your womb.</b> Even though it already said above ״בָּרוּךְ פְּרִי בִטְנְךָ״ — since the first promise corresponds to Torah study and this promise corresponds to the observance of the commandments, it was necessary to specify the reward for each one, for one is not learned from the other. And it was precise in saying <b>וְהוֹתִירְךָ ה׳ לְטוֹבָה</b> and did not say ״בָּרוּךְ פְּרִי בִטְנְךָ״ — because this promise is a good measure, directed against the observance of negative commandments, and the intention is that since by virtue of a person guarding himself from transgressions the forces of evil will not cleave to him — for from this it would be caused that the children issuing from him would be of the aspect of evil — and see what we have written at length in parashat Vayechi on the verse ״רְאוּבֵן בְּכֹרִי אַתָּה״ (Genesis 49:3), and may the matters be pleasing to you. Therefore it says in the promise corresponding to the observance of negative commandments: <b>וְהוֹתִירְךָ ה׳ לְטוֹבָה בִּפְרִי בִטְנְךָ</b> — that the children who remain after you will be of a good measure and not of an evil measure." "And its saying <b>in the fruit of your cattle and in the fruit of your land</b> — to say that from the side of the observance of negative commandments these two things will be sustained in his hand, just as they were promised as reward for Torah study as written above."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:19:43.117655069Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}